<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-adc">
    <title>Analog-to-Digital Conversion</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to read a potentiometer with the
            STM32C031C6's own analog-to-digital converter, configuring it
            ourselves register by register.
            Most of the sensors you will use in this course report their measurement as a
            voltage: a temperature, a light level, the angle of a knob.  The
            STM32C031C6 computes only with integers.  The
            <term>Analog-to-Digital Converter</term> (ADC) is the bridge between
            the two, and it is the peripheral that turns continuous signals that measure the world into digital signals comprehensible to our processor.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day, and one reading that comes before
            it.  The reading works out what an ADC's number means and how the
            converter arrives at it; in class we'll wire the potentiometer and get
            a count out of it.  <xref ref="sec-adc-reference"/> collects the
            hardware details we'll want to look up later.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain what an ADC count means, and convert between counts and volts in both directions.</li>
                <li>Use the datasheet to find which pins support analog input and which ADC channel each one maps to.</li>
                <li>Wire a potentiometer as an adjustable voltage divider and predict the voltage at its wiper.</li>
                <li>Configure the ADC at the register level, then take and read an on-demand sample.</li>
                <li>Explain why a status register uses write-1-to-clear, and what a read-modify-write would destroy.</li>
                <li>Trace a successive-approximation conversion one bit at a time.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!--  BEFORE CLASS                                                -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <section xml:id="sec-adc-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: From Voltage to Number</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This reading builds the ideas we'll use in class: what the
                number coming out of an ADC actually represents, how to get a
                voltage you can vary by hand, and how the converter arrives at its
                answer.  You can read all of it away from your board, since in
                class we'll do the wiring together and work through the code and
                the registers.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-signal-chain">
            <title>The Sensor Signal Chain</title>
            <p>
                A physical quantity becomes a number your program can use by
                passing through a chain of stages.  A <term>sensor</term> converts
                the physical quantity (temperature, light, force) into a
                voltage.  That voltage is usually small and noisy, so an
                <term>analog front end</term> may amplify and filter it.  The ADC
                then measures the conditioned voltage and reports an integer, and
                the microcontroller, our STM32C031C6, computes with that integer.
            </p>
            <p>
                How much of this chain is inside the sensor varies, and it
                changes what your code has to do.  Some sensors are just the bare
                transducer: they hand you a small, raw voltage, and everything after
                it is yours to build.  Others include the analog front end, so what
                reaches your pin is already amplified and filtered.  And some
                include the converter as well: they run the whole chain internally
                and hand you a <em>digital</em> result, which the microcontroller collects over
                a communication protocol rather than by converting anything itself.
                We'll see that third kind, and the communication protocols they use, later in
                the course.  This chapter is about the case where the converting is
                yours to do.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-signal-chain">
                <caption>The sensor signal chain.  A sensor converts a physical
                quantity into a voltage; an analog front end conditions that
                voltage by amplifying and filtering it; the ADC digitizes the
                result into an integer; the microcontroller processes the integer.  Every
                measurement you make in this course travels this path, and each
                stage can limit the quality of the final number.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-signal-chain.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The ADC is at the boundary between the continuous world and the
                discrete one: the voltage going in can take any value at all,
                while the number coming out is one of a fixed set of integers.
                In <xref ref="subsec-adc-quantization"/> we'll work out exactly
                what that costs us.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-quantization">
            <title>Quantization: What the Number Means</title>
            <p>
                The top of an ADC's input range is set by a <term>reference
                voltage</term>, written <m>V_\text{ref}</m>.  On the Nucleo board
                <m>V_\text{ref}</m> is wired to the 3.3 V supply, so an input of
                0 V reads as count 0 and an input at <m>V_\text{ref}</m> reads at
                the top of the scale.  Every count in this section is a fraction
                of <m>V_\text{ref}</m>, so every conversion we do depends on that
                one number.
            </p>
            <p>
                An ADC divides its input range into a finite number of equal steps
                and reports which step the input falls into.  How many steps it has
                is set by its <term>resolution</term>, expressed in bits.  A
                <m>B</m>-bit ADC produces <m>2^B</m> distinct output values, mapping
                the input range <m>[0,\, V_\text{ref}]</m> onto the integers
                <m>0, 1, 2, \ldots, 2^B - 1</m>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-system-view">
                <caption>The ADC as a system.  An analog voltage between 0 and
                <m>V_\text{ref}</m> goes in, and a bundle of
                <m>B</m> wires carries the digital result out, as
                an integer between 0 and <m>2^B - 1</m>.  On the STM32C031C6,
                <m>V_\text{ref}</m> is 3.3 V and <m>B</m> can be 6, 8, 10, or 12.
                We start with 12, and you will change it later.  The relationship written across the figure,
                <m>D_\text{out} = \lfloor 4096 \cdot V_\text{in} / V_\text{ref} \rfloor</m>,
                is the whole conversion in one line, and it holds everywhere
                except at the very top of the range, where the result is limited to
                the largest count the converter has, 4095.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-system-view.svg" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-system-view" ref="fig-adc-system-view">
    <ul>
        <li>An analog voltage between 0 and <m>V_\text{ref}</m> goes in, and a <term>count</term> comes out.</li>
        <li><m>B</m> wires out &#8594; <m>2^B</m> possible values. <m>B = 12</m> gives 4096 of them.</li>
        <li><m>V_\text{ref}</m> sets the top of the range. On our board it is the 3.3 V supply.</li>
        <li><m>D_\text{out} = \lfloor 4096 \cdot V_\text{in} / V_\text{ref} \rfloor</m>, the whole conversion in one line.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>4096 steps, but the top one is numbered 4095, so full scale reads 4095, not 4096.</caption>
</slide>

            <p>
                When we choose <m>B = 12</m> in our ADC this gives us 4096 steps across the 0–3.3 V
                range.  One step (also called <term>LSB</term>, for least significant bit)
                is therefore worth
                <md number="no">
                    <mrow>1\text{ LSB} = \frac{V_\text{ref}}{2^B} = \frac{3.3\text{ V}}{4096} \approx 0.806\text{ mV}</mrow>
                </md>
                and to convert a count back into a voltage you multiply:
                <md number="no">
                    <mrow>V = \text{count} \times \frac{V_\text{ref}}{2^B} = \text{count} \times \frac{3.3\text{ V}}{4096}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Here is an example:  A count of 2048 corresponds to
                <m>2048 \times 3.3/4096 = 1.65\text{ V}</m>, exactly half of
                <m>V_\text{ref}</m>, which is what you would hope for the middle
                count.  Going the other way, an input of 1.0 V should produce
                <m>\lfloor 1.0 \times 4096/3.3 \rfloor = 1241</m> counts.
            </p>
            <p>
                Notice the floor.  The ADC cannot represent a voltage exactly; it
                reports the step the input fell into, so the result is always
                rounded down to a multiple of one LSB.  The largest count, 4095,
                corresponds to <m>4095 \times 3.3/4096 \approx 3.299\text{ V}</m>,
                not 3.3 V: there are 4096 steps, but the highest one is numbered
                4095.  Dividing by 4095 instead of 4096, or expecting the top count
                to read exactly <m>V_\text{ref}</m>, is an easy slip to make.
            </p>
            <p>
                The reason the divisor is 4096 is worth being precise about, because
                it is where that slip comes from.  The converter is not placing your
                voltage on a line between two endpoints; it is chopping the range
                into 4096 equal <term>bins</term>, each one LSB wide, and reporting
                which bin the voltage fell into.  Bin 0 covers 0 V up to one LSB,
                bin 1 the next LSB, and so on up to bin 4095, which covers the
                voltages just below <m>V_\text{ref}</m>.  So the count is a bin
                number rather than a proportional position, 4096 is the number of
                bins rather than the number of gaps between them, and the top count
                stands for a range of voltages rather than for
                <m>V_\text{ref}</m> exactly.
            </p>
            <p>
                More bits means smaller steps and less rounding error, at the cost
                of a slightly longer conversion: about two ADC clock cycles, some
                167 ns, for every two bits you keep.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-potentiometer">
            <title>The Potentiometer: A Voltage You Can Turn</title>
            <p>
                To explore an ADC you need an input voltage you can vary at will.
                The simplest source is a <term>potentiometer</term>, a resistor
                with a sliding contact.
            </p>
            <p>
                A potentiometer has three terminals.  The two outer ones connect to
                the ends of a fixed resistive track; the middle one, the
                <term>wiper</term>, connects to a contact that slides along that
                track as you turn the knob.  Connecting the outer terminals across
                a supply makes the device an adjustable
                <term>voltage divider</term>.  The wiper splits the track into two
                resistances: call <m>R_2</m> the resistance between the wiper and
                the terminal you have grounded, and <m>R_1</m> the resistance
                between the wiper and the terminal at the supply.  The wiper
                voltage is then
                <md number="no">
                    <mrow>V_\text{wiper} = V_\text{supply} \times \frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-divider">
                <caption>A potentiometer wired as an adjustable voltage divider.
                The fixed resistive track runs between 3.3 V and GND; the wiper
                taps it somewhere in between, splitting it into <m>R_1</m> above
                and <m>R_2</m> below.  Turning the knob slides the wiper, moving
                resistance from one side to the other while their sum stays fixed,
                so the wiper voltage sweeps continuously between the two rails.  At
                the halfway point <m>R_1 = R_2</m> and the wiper sits at half the
                supply.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-divider.svg" width="82%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Turning the knob shifts resistance from one side of the wiper to
                the other while their sum stays fixed, so the wiper voltage sweeps
                continuously from one supply rail to the other.  With the outer
                terminals on 3.3 V and GND, the wiper delivers anything from 0 V to
                3.3 V.  At the midpoint <m>R_1 = R_2</m> and the wiper sits at half
                the supply, 1.65 V.
            </p>
            <p>
                Here is an example with real numbers.  On a 10 kΩ potentiometer with
                the wiper a quarter of the way up from the grounded end,
                <m>R_2 = 2.5\text{ k}\Omega</m> and <m>R_1 = 7.5\text{ k}\Omega</m>,
                so <m>V_\text{wiper} = 3.3 \times 2500/(2500+7500) = 0.825\text{ V}</m>,
                a quarter of the supply, as the geometry suggests.  We use 10 kΩ
                mainly because it draws only a third of a milliamp across the
                rails.  A sensor's own resistance can also limit how quickly the ADC
                is able to measure it, and
                <xref ref="subsec-adc-ref-impedance"/> works out how large that
                resistance is allowed to be.
            </p>
            <p>
                The wiper is a <em>source</em> of voltage, not a control signal:
                you wire it to the pin you want to measure, and the ADC reads
                whatever voltage the knob is currently producing.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-successive-approx">
            <title>Successive Approximation: How the Answer Is Found</title>
            <p>
                Our ADC finds its answer by <term>successive approximation</term>,
                which is a binary search carried out in hardware.  Rather than
                comparing the input against all 4096 possible levels, it determines
                the result one bit at a time, starting from the most significant bit.
            </p>
            <p>
                Inside the ADC converter are a <term>DAC</term> (digital-to-analog
                converter) and a comparator.  The converter keeps a register (the
                <term>successive approximation register</term>, or SAR) holding
                its answer so far.  At each step it sets the next bit of the SAR,
                the DAC turns the SAR's current value into a test voltage, and the
                comparator reports whether the input is above or below it.  If the
                input is above, the bit stays set; if below, it is cleared.  After
                <m>B</m> comparisons the SAR holds the result.
            </p>
            <p>
                One more part is needed: a <term>sample-and-hold</term>
                circuit at the input captures the voltage at the instant the
                conversion begins and holds it steady for the converter to work
                against, even if the input voltage changes in the meantime.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-sar-block">
                <caption>Inside a successive-approximation ADC.  The SAR holds the
                answer under construction and drives the DAC, which converts that
                value into a test voltage using <m>V_\text{ref}</m>.  The diagram is
                drawn for our 12-bit converter: the SAR holds bits <m>b_{11}</m>
                down to <m>b_0</m>, so the search takes 12 comparisons.  The
                comparator compares the held input against the test voltage and
                feeds its verdict, <m>V_c</m>, back to the SAR, which keeps or
                clears the bit it is currently testing and moves on to the next.
                The sample-and-hold circuit on the input freezes
                <m>V_\text{in}</m> at the instant the conversion starts.  The clock
                input on the left paces the search: one comparison, and so one bit
                of the answer, per clock.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-sar-block.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-sar-block" ref="fig-adc-sar-block">
                <caption>You were the comparator. The number you were narrowing down is the SAR; the DAC is what turns that number into a test voltage to compare against. The sample-and-hold freezes the input when the conversion starts, which is why the pin may change while it runs.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                That is why the voltage on the pin may change freely during a
                conversion without corrupting the result: the converter is working
                from its snapshot, not from the live input.
            </p>
            <p>
                Because each bit costs one comparison, a conversion takes many
                clock cycles rather than happening instantly.  Your code must
                therefore <em>wait</em> for the converter to finish before reading
                the result.  The exact mechanism is one of the things we'll explore in class.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-adc-before-class">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-adc-lsb">
                <statement><p>A 12-bit ADC has <m>V_\text{ref} = 3.3\text{ V}</m>.
                What voltage does one LSB represent, and what is the maximum
                representable voltage?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>One LSB ≈ 0.81 mV; maximum representable
                        voltage ≈ 3.299 V (count 4095).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  One LSB = 3.3 V / 4096 ≈ 0.806 mV.
                        Count 4095 gives <m>4095 \times 3.3/4096 \approx 3.299\text{ V}</m>,
                        not quite 3.3 V, because there are 4096 steps but the
                        highest is numbered 4095.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>One LSB ≈ 0.81 mV; maximum representable
                        voltage = 3.3 V (count 4096).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The LSB value is right, but a 12-bit ADC
                        produces counts 0–4095.  Count 4096 does not exist, since it
                        would need a 13th bit.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>One LSB = 3.3 V; maximum = 4095 V.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The LSB is a small fraction of
                        <m>V_\text{ref}</m>, not equal to it, and the maximum
                        <em>output</em> is a count rather than a voltage.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>One LSB = 12 mV; maximum representable
                        voltage = 3.3 V.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>About 12 mV would be the LSB of an 8-bit ADC
                        (3.3 V / 256 ≈ 12.9 mV).  With 12 bits the step is much
                        smaller.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-adc-count-to-volts">
                <statement><p>Your ADC reports a count of 1241 with
                <m>V_\text{ref} = 3.3\text{ V}</m> and 12-bit resolution.  What
                voltage is on the pin, to the nearest 0.01 V?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>About 1.00 V</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct:
                        <m>1241 \times 3.3/4096 \approx 0.9998\text{ V}</m>.  This is
                        the conversion you will do constantly: count times
                        <m>V_\text{ref}</m>, divided by 4096.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 1.24 V</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>This reads the count as millivolts directly.
                        The count is a number of steps; each step is worth about
                        0.806 mV, not 1 mV.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 0.30 V</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>This divides instead of multiplying.  A count
                        well below half of 4095 must give a voltage well below half
                        of 3.3 V, but not that far below.  Check the direction of
                        the ratio.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 3.30 V</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>3.3 V corresponds to the top of the range,
                        count 4095.  A count of 1241 is roughly 30% of full
                        scale.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-adc-pot-wiper">
                <statement><p>A 10 kΩ potentiometer has its outer terminals wired
                to 3.3 V and GND.  You turn the knob until the wiper is one quarter
                of the way up from the GND end.  What voltage does the ADC see, and
                roughly what count?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>About 0.83 V, giving a count near 1024.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A quarter of the way up the track
                        puts the wiper at a quarter of the supply,
                        <m>3.3/4 = 0.825\text{ V}</m>, which is a quarter of full
                        scale: <m>4096/4 = 1024</m> counts.  Fractions of the track
                        map directly onto fractions of full scale.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 2.48 V, giving a count near 3072.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is three quarters of the supply, the
                        reading you would get a quarter of the way down from the
                        3.3 V end.  Check which terminal the wiper is near.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 0.83 V, giving a count near 830.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The voltage is right, but the count is not
                        millivolts.  Divide by the LSB size:
                        <m>0.825 / 0.000806 \approx 1024</m>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>2.5 kΩ, which the ADC reads directly as
                        resistance.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The ADC measures voltage only.  The
                        potentiometer's resistance matters because it forms a
                        divider, but what reaches the pin, and what is converted,
                        is a voltage.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-adc-wait">
                <statement><p>You start a conversion by writing to the control
                register, then immediately read the result register.  The value is
                wrong or stale regardless of the input voltage.  Why?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The conversion is not finished yet; the result
                        register does not hold this conversion's answer until the
                        converter signals that it is done.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Successive approximation needs one
                        comparison per bit, so the answer takes many clock cycles to
                        appear.  Code must wait for the end-of-conversion signal
                        before reading.  You will write exactly that wait in
                        class.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The pin must be configured as an output before
                        a conversion can start.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The opposite: the pin must be in
                        <em>analog</em> mode so the converter can reach the voltage
                        on it.  Driving it as an output would fight whatever is
                        connected.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The result is a floating-point value that must
                        be cast before it can be interpreted.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The result register holds an unsigned integer
                        count between 0 and 4095.  No floating point is involved
                        unless you choose to convert counts to volts.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The ADC cannot measure voltages near 0 V.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>0 V is a perfectly valid input and produces a
                        count of 0.  The problem here is timing, not the input
                        level.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-adc-sar-steps">
                <statement><p>A 3-bit successive-approximation ADC is measuring an
                input.  How many comparisons does it make to produce a complete
                result?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>3: one per bit, starting from the most
                        significant.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Successive approximation is a binary
                        search: each comparison resolves exactly one bit, so an
                        <m>N</m>-bit result needs <m>N</m> comparisons.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>7: one for each possible output value minus
                        one.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That describes a <em>flash</em> ADC, which uses
                        <m>2^N - 1</m> comparators simultaneously and gets its answer
                        in one step.  It is much faster and much larger; successive
                        approximation trades time for silicon.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>8: one for each possible output value.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>8 is the number of possible outputs of a 3-bit
                        converter, not the number of comparisons needed to choose
                        between them.  A binary search over 8 values takes 3
                        steps.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>1: the comparator resolves the result in a
                        single step.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>One comparison tells you only whether the input
                        is above or below one test voltage.  That is a single bit.
                        Three bits need three comparisons.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>

        <!--
        CANVAS QUIZ: "Before class — Analog-to-Digital Conversion"
        (Due 1 hour before class; auto-graded multiple choice; low stakes.)

        Q1. A 12-bit ADC with VREF = 3.3 V returns a count of 1024. What is the
            corresponding input voltage?
            A) 1.024 V
            B) Approximately 0.825 V  <- CORRECT  (1024 x 3.3/4096)
            C) Approximately 1.65 V
            D) Approximately 3.3 V

        Q2. Increasing resolution from 10 bits to 12 bits, with VREF unchanged:
            A) Increases the maximum measurable voltage.
            B) Makes each LSB smaller, reducing quantization error.  <- CORRECT
            C) Makes conversions faster.
            D) Has no effect on measurement accuracy.

        Q3. In a successive-approximation ADC, what generates the test voltages
            compared against the input?
            A) A voltage divider made from external resistors.
            B) An internal DAC.  <- CORRECT
            C) The GPIO pull-up resistor.
            D) The CPU's ALU.

        Q4. Why must the converted input remain stable during a conversion?
            A) The ADC draws large current that could disturb the source.
            B) The algorithm narrows down the answer bit by bit and assumes a fixed
               input; this is why a sample-and-hold is used.  <- CORRECT
            C) A changing voltage would damage the input circuitry.
            D) The result register can only update when the input is stable.

        Q5. A potentiometer's outer terminals are on 3.3 V and GND. The wiper is at
            the midpoint. What does a 12-bit ADC report?
            A) 0
            B) Approximately 2048  <- CORRECT
            C) Approximately 4095
            D) It depends on the potentiometer's resistance value.
        -->
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!--  DAY 7 IN CLASS                                              -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <section xml:id="sec-adc-day7">
        <title>Day 7 In-Class: Reading a Potentiometer</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll read a potentiometer with the ADC.  We'll start by
                working out what an ADC's count means, then use the datasheet to
                find which pins can be analog inputs and which channel each one
                reaches.  We'll then wire a potentiometer to one of those pins and
                configure the ADC register by register, so that the count printed
                on your terminal changes as you turn the knob.  Along the way
                we'll look closely at <c>ADC_ISR</c>, the ADC's status register, and
                at how a flag in it is cleared, which does not work the way
                clearing a bit has worked so far.  At the end we'll run a
                successive approximation by hand.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-meaning">
            <title>Part 1: What the Number Means</title>
            <p>
                An ADC turns a continuously varying input voltage into an
                integer, a <term>count</term>.  We'll start with what that count
                means: how to get from a voltage to a count, and back again.
                <xref ref="subsec-adc-quantization"/> works that relationship out
                in full, and we'll use it all day.  Commit to an answer before we
                discuss it.
            </p>
            <p>
                If it does not come back to you straight away, look back at
                <xref ref="subsec-adc-quantization"/>, and if you would rather sort
                it out properly after class, that is fine too: the next part starts
                from the datasheet and does not depend on this.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-adc-predict-count">
                <title>Predict the Count</title>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-predict-t1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The ADC is 12-bit with
                            <m>V_\text{ref} = 3.3\text{ V}</m>.  A voltage of
                            exactly 1.65 V is applied to the pin.  What count do you
                            expect?  Note the number.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-predict-t2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Now the other direction: the ADC reports 3072.  What
                            voltage is on the pin?  Give your answer in volts to two
                            decimal places, and state what one count is worth in
                            millivolts.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-meaning" room="yes">
    <p>The count is a number of <term>steps</term>, not a voltage.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>One step, <term>1 LSB</term>, is <m>V_\text{ref}/2^B = 3.3\text{ V}/4096 \approx 0.806\text{ mV}</m>.</li>
        <li><m>\text{volts} = \text{count} \times 3.3/4096</m>, and <m>\text{count} = \lfloor \text{volts} \times 4096/3.3 \rfloor</m>.</li>
        <li><p>Examples:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Count 2048 gives <m>2048 \times 3.3/4096 = 1.650\text{ V}</m>, exactly half of <m>V_\text{ref}</m>.</li>
                <li>1.0 V gives <m>\lfloor 1.0 \times 4096/3.3 \rfloor = 1241</m> counts.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>The count is a <term>bin number</term>: the range is chopped into 4096 equal bins, so we divide by <m>4096</m> and not by 4095. The top bin is number 4095, and it covers the voltages just below <m>V_\text{ref}</m>, which is why full scale reads 3.299 V.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <!-- Instructor-only, from her comment of 2026-09-15: the bins-versus-gaps
                 argument, for when a student asks why the scale factor is 4096 and
                 not 4095.  Her text, condensed to slide shape and split across two
                 slides because it ran 165px past the frame on one. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-bins-not-gaps" instructor="yes">
    <p><term>Why 4096 and not 4095?</term> An ADC is not drawing a line between two endpoints. It is chopping a continuous range into 4096 equal-sized bins, and 4096 is the number of bins, not the number of gaps between them.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>Where the 4095 intuition comes from.</term> Reading it as linear interpolation, <q>0 V maps to code 0 and 3.3 V maps to code 4095, so scale by 4095/3.3</q>, treats this like two endpoints on a ruler with 4096 tick marks and 4095 gaps between them. That is a reasonable model for mapping a value between two known reference points, but it is not what a quantizer does.</li>
        <li><term>What the ADC actually does.</term> A 12-bit ADC divides the full-scale range into <m>2^{12} = 4096</m> equal-width bins, each of width <m>\text{LSB} = V_\text{ref}/4096</m>. Bin 0 covers <m>[0, \text{LSB})</m>, bin 1 covers <m>[\text{LSB}, 2\,\text{LSB})</m>, and so on up through bin 4095, which covers <m>[4095\,\text{LSB}, V_\text{ref})</m>. The code tells you which bin the voltage fell into, <m>\text{code} = \lfloor V/\text{LSB} \rfloor = \lfloor V \times 4096/V_\text{ref} \rfloor</m>, so the count is a bin index and not a proportional position between two endpoints.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Hold this until somebody asks, or until the 4095/4096 slip appears in their code.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-bins-top-end" instructor="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><term>Why it matters at the top end.</term> Code 4095 is not a single point at 3.3 V; it is a whole range of voltages just below it. Exactly 3.3 V is the one value with no home, since it would need a 4097th bin edge, so real converters saturate at 4095 for anything at or above <m>(4095/4096)\,V_\text{ref}</m>. The endpoint model would imply that only exactly 3.3 V produces code 4095, and that each code stands for a single voltage rather than a range, which misrepresents quantization error: it is spread uniformly over <m>\pm\tfrac{1}{2}</m> LSB, not concentrated on a discrete set of exact points.</li>
        <li><term>The rule of thumb.</term> Dividing a range into <m>N</m> equal steps between two known endpoints, as on a ruler or in a lookup table with defined start and end values, uses <m>N-1</m> as the scale factor. Quantizing a continuous range into <m>N</m> bins, as an ADC does, uses <m>N</m>, because <m>N</m> counts bins and not gaps. An ADC is squarely the second case.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-datasheet">
            <title>Part 2: Which Pins Can Do Analog?</title>
            <p>
                Before we can measure anything we need to know which pin to
                connect the potentiometer to.  The STM32C031C6 has one ADC, and a
                multiplexer in front of it that can connect the converter to one
                of up to 23 <term>channels</term>, some of them external pins and
                some of them internal signals.  Not every pin is wired to a
                channel, and the only authority on which ones are is the
                datasheet.
            </p>
            <p>
                You looked up D4 in the Nucleo pinout when you moved the LED to a
                new pin, and you used <em>Table 13, Port A alternate function
                mapping</em> to find which pins USART2 could use.  Today's question is answered one
                table earlier in the same document.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-datasheet-table12">
                <caption>How to read <em>Table 12, Pin assignment and
                description</em>, in the <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">STM32C031C6 datasheet</url>.  The header row
                names the columns: the six on the left give the pin number in each
                package variant (ours is the 48-pin LQFP, the low-profile quad
                flat package), then the highlighted
                <em>Pin name</em> column, and on the far right the highlighted
                <em>Additional functions</em> column, the one that answers today's
                question.  Read that column three ways.  Where it names
                <c>ADC_INn</c>, as it does for <c>PA1</c>, <c>PA2</c> and
                <c>PA3</c>, the pin can be an analog input on channel <m>n</m>.
                Where it shows a dash, as for <c>PF3</c> and <c>VREF+</c>, the pin
                has no additional functions at all.  And where it names something
                that is not an <c>ADC_IN</c> (<c>PC14</c>'s <c>OSCX_IN</c>, or
                <c>PF2-NRST</c>'s <c>NRST</c>), the pin does have an additional
                function, just not an analog one.  Rows are skipped where marked,
                and the row you actually need is not among those shown.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-datasheet-table12.svg" width="92%"/>
            </figure>

            <activity xml:id="act-adc-datasheet-lookup">
                <title>Find Your Channel</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Open the STM32C031C6
                        datasheet and find Table 12, <em>Pin assignment and
                        description</em>.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-ds-t1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the row for <c>PA0</c>.  Read across to the
                            <em>Additional functions</em> column.  What does it say?
                            Does PA0 support analog input, and if so, on which channel?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-ds-t2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the row for <c>PA9</c>.  Can PA9 be used as an
                            analog input?  How do you know?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-ds-t3">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The pin you will use today is labeled <c>A0</c> on the
                            Nucleo's header, printed on the board beside the pin.
                            Note all three names for
                            it: the Arduino label, the port pin, and the ADC
                            channel number.  Keep these.  You will need the same
                            three-way translation for a different pin later.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                The three numbers happen to agree for A0: it is PA0, and it is
                channel 0.  That is a coincidence.  The header label, the port pin
                and the ADC channel are three independent naming schemes, and for
                most pins they do not agree, so we'll look each one up rather than
                assuming that one follows from the others.
            </p>

            <p>
                Which channel the converter actually samples is selected by the
                <term>channel selection register</term>, <c>ADC_CHSELR</c>.  Each
                bit enables one channel: set bit <m>n</m> to sample channel
                <m>n</m>.  (That plain reading holds because <c>CHSELRMOD</c> is 0,
                its reset value; the register can also be put into a different
                sequencing mode, which we do not use.)
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-chselr">
                <caption>The ADC's input multiplexer, from <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url> Figure 33.  The
                green labels mark the six port pins that the Nucleo brings out to
                the Arduino analog header.  Most of the other numbered channels do
                reach package pins too, and Table 12 is what tells you which.  Four
                channels reach no pin at all: <c>VSENSE</c>, <c>VREFINT</c>,
                <c>VDDA</c> and <c>VSSA</c> are internal signals (among them the
                on-chip temperature sensor and the internal reference) that this
                same ADC can measure with no external wiring whatsoever.  Bit
                <m>n</m> of <c>ADC_CHSELR</c> selects channel <m>n</m>
                (RM0490 §14.12.9).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-chselr.svg" width="60%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-table12" ref="fig-adc-datasheet-table12">
                <caption>Datasheet Table 12: read the Additional functions column three ways. ADC_IN<em>n</em> (PA1, PA2, PA3) means analog on channel <em>n</em>; a dash (PF3, VREF+) means no additional function at all; anything else (OSCX_IN, NRST) means it has one, but not an analog one.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-three-names">
                <ul>
                    <li><term>[header]</term> <clr c="orange">A0</clr>, the label printed on the board beside the pin.</li>
                    <li><term>[port pin]</term> <clr c="blue">PA0</clr>, what <c>MODER</c> names.</li>
                    <li><term>[channel]</term> <clr c="green">Channel 0</clr>, what <c>CHSELR</c> names.</li>
                    <li>All three happen to agree for A0, and for most other pins they do not, so look up each one.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-chselr" ref="fig-adc-chselr">
                <caption>Bit <em>n</em> of CHSELR selects channel <em>n</em>. Note VSENSE and VREFINT, internal signals the same ADC can measure with no wiring at all.</caption>
            </slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-circuit">
            <title>Part 3: The Test Circuit</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll build the test circuit we'll measure for the rest of
                the day: a potentiometer wired across the supply, with its wiper
                feeding the pin we just looked up.  Turning the knob sweeps that
                pin's voltage between the two rails, which is what gives the ADC
                something to convert.
            </p>
            <p>
                You may be able to push the potentiometer's pins (hard) straight
                into the breadboard.  Otherwise, use three of the pin-to-clip
                jumpers.  Either way the connections are the same: the two outer
                terminals go to 3.3 V and GND, and the wiper, the middle terminal,
                goes to the pin labeled <c>A0</c>.  Watch which leg is which: 3.3 V
                and GND on the wiper and one outer leg puts the two rails across
                part of the track only, which is close to a short at one end of the
                knob's travel.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-pot-photo">
                <caption>The potentiometer itself, with the three terminals
                identified beneath it: GND and 3.3 V go to the outer two, and the
                wiper, the middle terminal, goes to A0. </caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-pot-photo.svg" width="45%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-test-circuit">
                <caption>The test circuit.  The potentiometer's fixed track runs
                between 3.3 V and GND, and the wiper taps off a voltage somewhere
                between them, set by the knob.  That wiper voltage is the analog
                input to PA0.  This is the same circuit you will use for the first
                part of Lab 4.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-test-circuit.svg" width="35%"/>
            </figure>

            <activity xml:id="act-adc-wire-measure">
                <title>Predict, Then Measure</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Put your digital multimeter (DMM) on the wiper, and answer
                        the questions in order, predicting first and measuring
                        afterwards.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-wire-t1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Predict the wiper voltage with the knob turned fully
                            counter-clockwise, at its center, fully clockwise, and
                            about a quarter of the way up from the counter-clockwise
                            end.  Note all four numbers before you measure
                            anything.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-wire-t2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Now measure all four with the DMM.  Where do your
                            predictions and measurements disagree, and by how much?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-wire-t3">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Convert each of your four measured voltages into the
                            count you expect the ADC to report.  Keep these
                            numbers, because we'll check them against the ADC's own
                            readings once the program is running.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-pot" ref="fig-adc-test-circuit">
    <ul>
        <li><term>Build this now.</term> The pot's track runs between 3.3 V and GND; the wiper taps off somewhere in between.</li>
        <li>Outer two terminals &#8594; <clr c="red">3.3 V</clr> and <clr c="blue">GND</clr>.</li>
        <li>Middle terminal (the <term>wiper</term>) &#8594; <clr c="green">A0</clr>.</li>
        <li>Push the pins hard into the breadboard, or use three pin-to-clip jumpers.</li>
        <li>Turning the knob sweeps the wiper voltage between the rails. That is what gives the ADC something to convert.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Watch the middle terminal: 3.3 V and GND on the wiper plus one outer leg is a near short at one end of travel.</caption>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-registers">
            <title>Part 4a: The Registers, One at a Time</title>
            <p>
                Reading one voltage takes five steps: set the pin's mode, enable
                the ADC's clock, select the channel, start a conversion, and wait
                for it to finish.  Today we'll do each of those five steps
                ourselves.  Because you write all five, you can change any of them
                later, for a different pin, a different resolution or a faster
                sample, and you can see where a measurement went wrong when it
                does.  We'll look at each step on its own before you write any of
                them.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>The pin.</term>  An analog input is a fourth GPIO mode,
                alongside input, output, and alternate function.  Its <c>MODER</c>
                encoding is <c>0b11</c>.  Like every peripheral, a GPIO port needs
                its own clock enabled before its registers respond, and note that
                this is the <em>port's</em> clock, in <c>RCC_IOPENR</c>, which is a
                different register from the ADC's own clock enable that follows.  Then, as
                always with a multi-bit field, clear it before setting it: writing
                only the set would leave whatever was there before ORed in.  The
                example below uses <c>PA4</c>, not the <c>PA0</c> you actually need,
                so that you have to work out which parts change rather than copying
                it across:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->IOPENR  |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;                     // clock to port A
GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE4_Msk;                  // clear both mode bits
GPIOA->MODER |=  (GPIO_ANALOG << GPIO_MODER_MODE4_Pos);  // then set them to 0b11
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                <c>GPIO_ANALOG</c> is the constant <c>3</c> that <c>ES28.h</c>
                defines for this mode, the same list of mode constants you used on
                Day 6 (<c>GPIO_INPUT</c> 0, <c>GPIO_OUTPUT</c> 1,
                <c>GPIO_ALTERNATE</c> 2, <c>GPIO_ANALOG</c> 3), and 3 is
                <c>0b11</c> in binary, which is what the two <c>MODER</c> bits must
                hold.  <c>GPIO_MODER_MODE4_Pos</c> is the name that CMSIS, the
                Common Microcontroller Software Interface Standard that ships with
                the project's headers, gives to the position where that two-bit
                field starts.  Using the named constants rather than counting bit
                positions by hand is what keeps this readable.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>The ADC clock.</term>  Like every peripheral, the ADC does
                nothing until its clock is enabled.  The ADC is on the APB, the
                Advanced Peripheral Bus, and its enable bit is in
                <c>RCC_APBENR2</c>:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_ADCEN;   // enable the clock to the ADC
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The converter also has its own conversion clock, asynchronous to
                the APB bus clock that this line enables.  We leave it at its
                default source and prescaler, so there is nothing further to
                configure today, but remember that this line is where the ADC
                becomes reachable at all.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>The channel.</term>  Set the bit for the channel you want in
                <c>ADC_CHSELR</c>.  To sample channel 4, which is where
                <c>PA4</c> appears:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
ADC1->CHSELR |= ADC_CHSELR_CHSEL4;   // select channel 4
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The peripheral is called <c>ADC1</c> even though this chip has only
                one converter: other members of the STM32 family have several, and
                the name is kept consistent so code ports between them.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>The converter.</term>  Two registers govern the ADC itself: <c>ADC_CR</c>
                and <c>ADC_ISR</c>. Of these two,
                <c>ADC_CR</c> is the control register. In this register, <c>ADEN</c> enables the ADC
                and <c>ADSTART</c> begins a conversion.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-cr">
                <caption>The ADC control register, <c>ADC_CR</c>.  Bit 0
                (<c>ADEN</c>) enables the converter; bit 2 (<c>ADSTART</c>) starts a
                conversion and is cleared by hardware when that conversion
                completes.  You set these bits; the hardware manages the
                rest.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-cr-annotated.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Enabling the ADC does not make it usable immediately, since the analog
                circuitry needs time to stabilize.  The three steps to help accomplish
                this are: clear <c>ADRDY</c>, set <c>ADEN</c>, and then wait for
                <c>ADRDY</c> to come back.  That flag is how the hardware tells you
                the converter is ready to accept work.  Note that the first two
                happen once, in our initialization routine <c>pa0_adc_init()</c>,
                while the wait belongs in <c>start_conversion()</c>, which we call
                each time we want to take a sample.
            </p>
            <p>
                <c>ADC_ISR</c> is the interrupt and status register, where the
                hardware reports what it has done.  Two of its bits matter today:
                <c>ADRDY</c>, set when the converter has finished starting up and is
                ready to begin a conversion, and <c>EOC</c>, set when a conversion has finished and the
                result is available.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-isr">
                <caption>The ADC interrupt and status register, <c>ADC_ISR</c>.
                <c>ADRDY</c> (bit 0) means the converter is ready to be used;
                <c>EOC</c> (bit 2) means a conversion has completed and its result
                is waiting in the data register.  Both are set by hardware.  Your
                code's job is to wait for them.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-isr-annotated.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Waiting for a status bit is a pattern you have used before.  On
                Day 5 you waited for the UART to be ready to accept a character:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (!(USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_TXE_TXFNF)) {}   // Day 5: wait for the UART
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The ADC wait has the same shape: same <c>while</c>, same
                <c>!(register &amp; bit)</c>, a different register and a different
                bit.  Written out for the end-of-conversion flag, it is:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (!(ADC1->ISR & ADC_ISR_EOC)) {}    // wait until the conversion is done
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Keep looping for as long as it is <em>not</em> true that the flag
                bit is set.
            </p>

            <p>
                Finally, when a conversion completes, the 12-bit result appears in
                the data register <c>ADC_DR</c>, right-aligned in bits 11:0.
                Reading <c>ADC_DR</c> also clears <c>EOC</c>, so each result is
                collected exactly once.
            </p>
            <p>
                <c>ADC_DR</c> is a 32-bit register, but the result inside it is only
                12 bits wide and is at the bottom, so it fits comfortably in a
                <c>uint16_t</c>, which is why <c>adc_read()</c> returns one rather
                than a full <c>uint32_t</c>.  The cast <c>(uint16_t)</c> discards the
                upper 16 bits of the register, and that is safe here precisely
                because those bits are reserved and always read as zero.  Narrowing a
                value you have not checked is a good way to lose data silently; this
                one you have checked.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-registers-intro">
    <p>Reading one voltage takes <term>five</term> steps:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>set the pin's mode: <c>GPIOA-&gt;MODER</c></li>
        <li>enable the ADC's clock: <c>RCC-&gt;APBENR2</c></li>
        <li>select the channel: <c>ADC1-&gt;CHSELR</c></li>
        <li>start a conversion: <c>ADC1-&gt;CR</c></li>
        <li>wait for it to finish: <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR</c></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Because you write all five, you can <term>change</term>
    any of them later (a different pin, a different resolution, a faster sample), and you can see
    where a measurement went wrong when it does.</p>
</slide>

<slide xml:id="sl-adc-reg-pin">
    <p><term>1. Setting the pin to analog mode.</term></p>
    <p><clr c="red">This example uses PA4, not the pin you will be using.</clr>
    Work out which parts change for PA0 rather than copying it across.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->IOPENR  |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;                      // PA4 example
GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE4_Msk;                   // clear both mode bits
GPIOA->MODER |=  (GPIO_ANALOG << GPIO_MODER_MODE4_Pos);  // then set them to 0b11
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Each pin gets <term>two</term> MODER bits: 00 input, 01 output, 10 alternate, <clr c="green">11 analog</clr>.</li>
        <li><c>GPIO_ANALOG</c> is the ES28.h constant for 3, and 3 is <c>0b11</c>, the two bits we need.</li>
        <li>Clear <em>then</em> set: a bare <c>|=</c> would leave whatever was already there ORed in.</li>
        <li><c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR</c> is the <term>port's</term> clock, not the ADC's, a different register from the one on the next slide.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-reg-clock">
    <p><term>2. The ADC's clock.</term> Like every peripheral, the ADC is unreachable until its clock is enabled.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_ADCEN;   // enable the clock to the ADC
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Without this line every ADC register reads back as 0 and every write is ignored.</li>
        <li>The converter also has its own conversion clock, asynchronous to this bus clock, and we leave it at its default.</li>
        <li>This is the one line that is the same whatever pin or channel you use.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-reg-channel">
    <p><term>3. The channel: which pin reaches the converter.</term></p>
    <p>We have only <term>one</term> ADC, but many pins could supply an analog signal.
    A <term>multiplexer</term> in front of the converter passes exactly one of
    those pins through to it, and <c>ADC_CHSELR</c> is how we say which one.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
ADC1->CHSELR |= ADC_CHSELR_CHSEL4;   // channel 4 -- again, an EXAMPLE, not your channel
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>One bit per channel: set bit <em>n</em> to select channel <em>n</em>.</li>
        <li>The channel number is <em>not</em> the pin number and <em>not</em> the header label: Table 12 connects them.</li>
        <li>Our chip's ADC is called <c>ADC1</c>, even though it is the only one; other STM32 parts have several, and the name keeps code portable.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-cr" ref="fig-adc-cr">
    <p><term>4. ADC_CR, the control register</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>A <term>control register</term> is how software tells the peripheral what to do. Two bits matter today.</li>
        <li><clr c="green">ADEN</clr> (bit 0) enables the ADC. Once, in init.</li>
        <li><clr c="orange">ADSTART</clr> (bit 2) starts one conversion. Every sample.</li>
        <li>Both are <c>rs</c>: you can read them and <term>set</term> them, but you cannot clear them, since the hardware does that.</li>
        <li>So ADSTART reading 1 means the converter is still busy; hardware clears it at the end.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-isr" ref="fig-adc-isr">
    <p><term>5. ADC_ISR, the interrupt and status register</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>A <term>status register</term> is the other direction: how the hardware keeps <em>you</em> updated on what is happening.</li>
        <li><clr c="green">ADRDY</clr> (bit 0): the converter has started up and will accept work.</li>
        <li><clr c="orange">EOC</clr> (bit 2): a conversion finished; the result is waiting in <c>ADC_DR</c>.</li>
        <li>Both are <c>rc_w1</c>: read, cleared by writing 1. Remember that; we come back to it.</li>
        <li>Your code never sets these. It <term>waits</term> for them.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-polling">
    <p>You already know how to wait for a status bit.  You did it for the UART.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (!(USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_TXE_TXFNF)) {}   // Day 5: wait for the UART

while (!(  PERIPH->ISR &  FLAG           )) {}    // the shape
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Keep looping <term>while it is not true</term> that the flag bit is set.</li>
        <li>The loop body is empty. All the work is the condition.</li>
        <li>Both of the waits you are about to write use this shape: the register is <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR</c>, and the two bit names are on the previous slide.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Do NOT fill these in &#8212; combining this slide with the ADC_ISR slide is the transfer.</note>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-code">
            <title>Part 4b: Writing ADCPot.c</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll put those five registers together.  The program has
                three parts: a one-time initialization, a function that starts a
                conversion, and a function that waits for and returns the result.
            </p>
            <p>
                Here is the skeleton.  Each <c>// TODO</c> is one of the clusters
                you have just seen, but for <em>channel 0 on PA0</em>, not the
                channel 4 examples above.
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* ADCPot.c -- read a potentiometer on PA0 with the ADC
 * ENGS 28
 */
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"

void     pa0_adc_init(void);
void     start_conversion(void);
uint16_t adc_read(void);

int main(void) {
    uint16_t sensor_value;
    uart2_init();

    printf("\n\rHello from STM32C0!\r\n");
    printf("Single conversion ADC test\r\n");

    pa0_adc_init();

    while (1) {
        start_conversion();
        sensor_value = adc_read();
        printf("Sensor value: %d\r\n", sensor_value);
        delay_ms(1000);
    }
    return 0;
}

void pa0_adc_init(void) {
    // TODO 1 -- configure PA0 as an analog input
    //           (enable the GPIOA clock, then clear and set the mode bits)

    // TODO 2 -- enable the clock to the ADC

    // TODO 3 -- select the channel PA0 is connected to

    // Enable the ADC module
    ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;   // clear the ready bit  (we'll come back to this)
    ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADEN;     // set the ADC enable bit
}

void start_conversion(void) {
    // TODO 4 -- wait until the ADC reports that it is ready (ADRDY)

    ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADSTART;  // start a single conversion
}

uint16_t adc_read(void) {
    // TODO 5 -- wait until the conversion has finished (EOC)

    return (uint16_t) ADC1->DR;  // reading DR also clears EOC
}
]]></code></program>

            <activity xml:id="act-adc-write-code">
                <title>Make the Knob Move the Number</title>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-code-t1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Fill in the five <c>// TODO</c> blanks.  Build, flash,
                            and open your serial terminal.  Turn the knob.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-code-t2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Compare what you see against the four counts you
                            predicted when you measured the wiper with the DMM.
                            Do they match?  If a reading is
                            off by more than a few counts, is the disagreement in
                            your prediction, your DMM measurement, or the ADC?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                <term>Nothing printed?</term>  Each of these symptoms has a
                different cause, so work down the list in order.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <term>The debugger will not connect, with a libusb error.</term>
                    Check that your power rail is on <c>3V3</c> and not
                    <c>NRST</c>.  The two are adjacent on the header, so a jumper
                    one row off is easy to make and easy to miss, and it is a common
                    wiring error in this class.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <term>No output at all, but the board flashes and runs.</term>
                    Confirm you are building the project you think you are.  A stray
                    copy of <c>blinky.c</c> left in the project will build and run
                    without error while printing nothing.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <term>Output appears but never changes.</term>  The wiper is
                    probably not connected to A0.  Failing that, check that TODO 1
                    set the mode bits to <c>0b11</c> and not to something else. Note 
                    that the reset value for <c>MODER</c> for most GPIO pins is 3 (analog mode)
                    so if you forgot this step entirely it's not the cause for this error.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <term>The value is always 0.</term>  The pin really is sitting at
                    0 V.  Check that the wiper jumper shares a breadboard row with
                    the pot's <em>middle</em> leg, and that the two outer legs go to
                    3.3 V and GND, not both to GND.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <term>The program hangs with no output after the greeting.</term>
                    One of the <c>while</c> loops is waiting for a bit that will
                    never be set.  All three likely causes look identical from
                    outside: the ADC clock (TODO 2), the channel (TODO 3), or the
                    ready wait (TODO 4).  Check them in that order.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                Finished early?  Turn the knob to each mechanical end stop.  Do you
                read exactly 0 and exactly 4095?  If not, why not, and is the
                explanation in the potentiometer or in the converter?
            </p>

            <p>
                Here is the same exercise in the simulator, for working on it away
                from your Nucleo.  Click <em>+ Pot</em> in the component bay, then
                click the Arduino header pin labeled <c>A0</c>, and drag the knob:
                the wiper voltage appears under the knob, and it is the number
                your DMM would read.  That one click wires the whole divider, so
                there is nothing to connect to 3.3 V or to ground.  The register
                panel shows <c>ADC1-&gt;CHSELR</c>, <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR</c> and
                <c>ADC1-&gt;DR</c> as soon as your program touches the converter,
                so you can watch <c>EOC</c> come up and the count land in
                <c>DR</c>.  One warning: the conversion is not timed here, so the
                sampling time and source resistance of
                <xref ref="subsec-adc-ref-impedance"/> have no effect on what you
                read, and the reading moves by about one count either way, which
                is less movement than a real converter and a real potentiometer
                will give you.  Nothing here is saved for you, so use the
                download button, marked <em>&#8595;</em>, to keep a copy of your
                work, or <em>Full screen</em>, which carries your edits into a new
                tab you can bookmark.
            </p>

            <sim starter="ADCPot" coolterm="yes" height="740"/>

            <!-- Split from sl-adc-skeleton: the set-up steps and the 17-line
                 skeleton together ran 127px past the bottom of the frame, and
                 this is the crucial step (P-2) — the one slide that must not
                 lose a line. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-setup">
    <ul>
        <li>Copy your TemplateProject and name the copy <c>ADCPot</c>.</li>
        <li>Download the <c>ADCPot.c</c> skeleton from Canvas into the project's <c>Src</c> folder.</li>
        <li>Then fill in the five blanks, using the five registers we just went through.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-skeleton">
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void pa0_adc_init(void) {
    // TODO 1 -- PA0 as analog input (GPIOA clock, then clear+set the mode bits)
    // TODO 2 -- enable the clock to the ADC
    // TODO 3 -- select the channel PA0 is connected to

    ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;   // clear the ready bit  (we'll come back to this)
    ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADEN;     // set the ADC enable bit
}

void start_conversion(void) {
    // TODO 4 -- wait until the ADC reports ready (ADRDY)
    ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADSTART;
}

uint16_t adc_read(void) {
    // TODO 5 -- wait until the conversion has finished (EOC)
    return (uint16_t) ADC1->DR;  // reading DR also clears EOC
}
]]></code></program>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-diagnostics">
                <p>Work down the list.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><term>libusb error, debugger won&apos;t connect</term>: power rail on <c>NRST</c>, not <c>3V3</c>.</li>
                    <li><term>Runs, but no output</term>: a stray <c>blinky.c</c> is what&apos;s building.</li>
                    <li><term>Output never changes</term>: the wiper is probably not on A0. (Most pins reset to analog mode, so a forgotten TODO 1 is not the cause.)</li>
                    <li><term>Always 0</term>: the pin really is at 0 V. Check the wiper shares a row with the pot's <em>middle</em> leg, and that the outer legs are not both on GND.</li>
                    <li><term>Hangs after the greeting</term>: waiting on a bit that will never be set.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>Leave this up for the whole of Part 4.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-solution" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void pa0_adc_init(void) {
    // Configure PA0 as analog input pin
    RCC->IOPENR  |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;                     // clock to GPIOA
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE0_Msk;                  // clear mode bits
    GPIOA->MODER |= (GPIO_ANALOG << GPIO_MODER_MODE0_Pos);  // analog (11)

    // Configure the ADC module
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_ADCEN;   // clock to the ADC
    ADC1->CHSELR |= ADC_CHSELR_CHSEL0;   // channel 0

    // Enable the ADC module
    ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;
    ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADEN;
}

void start_conversion(void) {
    while (!(ADC1->ISR & ADC_ISR_ADRDY)) {}
    ADC1->CR |= ADC_CR_ADSTART;
}

uint16_t adc_read(void) {
    while (!(ADC1->ISR & ADC_ISR_EOC)) {}      // blocking
    return (uint16_t) ADC1->DR;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-atomic">
            <title>Part 5: Wait, How Did That Clear the Bit?</title>
            <p>
                Look again at the line in <c>pa0_adc_init()</c> that clears the
                ready bit.  By everything we have done so far, it should not work:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;   // "clear the ready bit"
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Think back to Blinky.  When you wanted to clear a bit in a register,
                you did this:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LED_PIN (1U << 5)

GPIOA->ODR |=  LED_PIN;   // set bit 5   -- LED on
GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;   // clear bit 5 -- LED off
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Clearing meant ANDing with the complement of the mask.  But
                <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;</c> is a plain assignment, and
                <c>ADC_ISR_ADRDY</c> is a mask with bit 0 <em>set</em>.  Read
                literally, that line writes a 1 into bit 0 and a 0 into every other
                bit of the status register, the opposite of clearing bit 0, and
                apparently destroying everything else.
            </p>
            <p>
                And yet it is correct, and the <c>&amp;= ~</c> version would be a
                bug.  Why?
            </p>

            <p>
                The answer is that this register's bits do not behave like ordinary
                memory.  <c>ADRDY</c> is a <term>write-1-to-clear</term> bit,
                <c>rc_w1</c> in the <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">reference manual</url>'s notation.  For such a bit:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>writing a <c>1</c> clears it;</li>
                <li>writing a <c>0</c> does nothing at all.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                So the assignment does exactly what is wanted: bit 0 receives a 1
                and is cleared, while every other bit receives a 0 and is left
                untouched.  The register is not being overwritten; it is being sent
                one instruction per bit.
            </p>
            <p>
                Now consider what <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR &amp;= ~ADC_ISR_ADRDY;</c> would
                do.  The compiler has to read the whole register, clear one bit in
                the copy, and write all 32 bits back.  That write puts a
                <c>1</c> into every status bit that happened to be set when the read
                occurred, and on this register, writing a 1 means <em>clear</em>.
                So every other flag that was already raised at the moment of the
                read would be wiped out, including one you had not looked at yet and
                were about to handle.  You would lose events you never knew had
                happened.
            </p>
            <p>
                And it would not even do the job you asked for.  The point of the
                operation was to clear <c>ADRDY</c>, so the read-modify-write puts a
                <c>0</c> into that bit position, and writing a 0 to an
                <c>rc_w1</c> bit does nothing at all.  <c>ADRDY</c> would still be
                set.  The line manages to destroy the flags you cared about while
                leaving the one flag you meant to clear exactly as it was.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is why status registers are built this way.  A flag register
                is written by two parties (the hardware sets bits, your code
                clears them), and a read-modify-write cannot safely share a register
                with a writer it cannot see.  Write-1-to-clear removes the conflict:
                your code names the single bit it means to clear and says nothing
                about the others.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-rmw-damage">
                <caption>Why the read-modify-write is wrong twice over.  Start with
                three flags set.  A plain assignment writes a 1 into ADRDY and a 0
                into everything else, so ADRDY clears and the others are untouched.
                The <c>&amp;= ~</c> version reads all 32 bits, clears ADRDY in its
                copy, and writes the lot back, which sends a 1 to every flag that
                was already set, clearing OVR and EOC, while sending a 0 to ADRDY,
                which does nothing.  The flags you needed are gone and the one you
                meant to clear is still set.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-rmw-damage.svg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <insight xml:id="insight-adc-rc-w1">
                <title>Reading the access type</title>
                <p>
                    The reference manual labels every register field with how it may
                    be accessed: <c>rw</c> for ordinary read-write bits,
                    <c>r</c> for read-only, <c>rs</c> for bits you can
                    set but not clear (writing 0 does nothing, and <c>ADEN</c> and
                    <c>ADSTART</c> are both <c>rs</c>), <c>rc_w1</c> for read,
                    cleared by writing 1, and <c>rc_w0</c> for the opposite
                    convention, cleared by writing 0.  The digit in the name tells
                    you which value does the clearing, so it is worth reading rather
                    than assuming.  When you come across a status register you have
                    not used before, check the
                    access type before you decide how to clear a flag.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-wait-what">
                <p>This line is in your code, and it works:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
ADC1->ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;   // "clear the ready bit"
]]></code></program>
                <p>But in Blinky, clearing a bit looked like this:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;      // clear bit 5
]]></code></program>
                <p>The ADC line is a plain assignment of a mask with bit 0 <em>set</em>. How does that <em>clear</em> anything?</p>
                <note>Let them sit with it. Do not answer yet &#8212; the puzzlement is the teaching.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-rc-w1">
    <p><term>rc_w1</term>: read, cleared by writing 1.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Write a <clr c="green">1</clr> to the bit &#8594; it <term>clears</term>.</li>
        <li>Write a <clr c="blue">0</clr> to the bit &#8594; <term>nothing happens</term>. It keeps its value.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So <c>ADC1-&gt;ISR = ADC_ISR_ADRDY;</c> sends a 1 to bit 0 and a 0 to all the rest:
    bit 0 clears, every other bit is left exactly as it was.</p>
    <p>The register is not being overwritten. It is being sent one instruction per bit.</p>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-why-not-rmw" ref="fig-adc-rmw-damage" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><c>&amp;= ~</c> is a <term>read</term>, a modify, and a <term>write-back of all 32 bits</term>.</li>
        <li>That write-back sends a <clr c="red">1</clr> to every flag that was already set, and here 1 means <em>clear</em>. Those events are lost.</li>
        <li>And it sends a <clr c="orange">0</clr> to ADRDY, the bit you were trying to clear, which does nothing at all.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-sar">
            <title>Part 6: Successive Approximation, Live</title>
            <p>
                In the pre-class reading we saw that the converter finds its
                answer by a binary search, one bit at a time.  Let's run one
                ourselves.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-adc-guessing-game">
                <title>Be the Converter</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Think of a whole number between 0 and 15 (four bits' worth)
                        and keep it to yourself.  We will find it by successive
                        approximation, exactly as the hardware does, one bit at a
                        time from the most significant down.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-sar-t1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Answer only yes or no to each question.  Starting with
                            <c>SAR = 0b1000</c> (8), so testing the highest bit, namely
                            bit 3: is your number greater than or
                            equal to 8?  If yes, the bit stays; if no, it is cleared.
                            Then test bit 2, then bit 1, then bit 0 the same way.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-sar-t2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            How many questions did it take, and how many would it
                            take for a number between 0 and 4095?  What does that
                            tell you about how long a 12-bit conversion must take?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                Four bits took four questions; twelve bits take twelve comparisons,
                plus a little overhead for sampling, about 14 ADC clock cycles in
                total (<xref ref="fig-adc-timing"/>).  That is why a conversion is not instantaneous, and why your
                code has to wait for <c>EOC</c> rather than reading <c>ADC_DR</c>
                straight away.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-sar-game">
    <p>Think of a number from 0 to 15. Yes or no only.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
SAR = 0b1000  ->  is it >= 8 ?     no   ->  clear   0b0000
SAR = 0b0100  ->  is it >= 4 ?     yes  ->  keep    0b0100
SAR = 0b0110  ->  is it >= 6 ?     yes  ->  keep    0b0110
SAR = 0b0111  ->  is it >= 7 ?     yes  ->  keep    0b0111  = 7
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>4 bits took 4 questions. 12 bits takes <term>12 comparisons</term>, one per bit, always.</li>
        <li>Before it can compare anything it must <term>sample</term>: charge the internal capacitor to the input voltage. That costs 1.5 clocks.</li>
        <li>The 12 comparisons cost 12.5 clocks: the extra half cycle is the converter's own overhead, latching the finished result into <c>ADC_DR</c>.</li>
        <li><clr c="green">1.5 + 12.5 = 14</clr> ADC clocks for one 12-bit conversion.</li>
        <li>At 12 MHz that is about 1.17 microseconds, and it is why your code must wait for EOC.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-day7-homework">
            <title>Part 7: Before Next Class</title>
            <p>
                Tonight's homework continues directly into Lab 4, so keep a record
                of what you find.  Make one change at a time, and change it back
                before you make the next one.
            </p>

            <p>
                Moving the pot to A3 and changing the resolution can both be done
                in the simulator (<xref ref="subsec-adc-day7-code"/>) as well as
                on your board.  It takes its channel numbers from the same Table
                12 of the
                <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">STM32C031C6
                datasheet</url>, so the channel you look up is the one it will
                sample, and a write to <c>ADC_CFGR1</c> while <c>ADEN</c> is set
                is ignored there just as it is on the chip.  It does give you one
                of the three numbers: the port pin is printed beside every header
                pin on the simulated board, which your Nucleo does not print on
                its silkscreen.  The channel is still yours to find.
            </p>

            <p>
                For checking the arithmetic both ways you need your own DMM and
                your own potentiometer.  In the simulator the count and the wiper
                voltage under the knob are computed from the same number, so they
                are not two independent measurements, and agreement between them
                would tell you nothing.  If your kit is not with you tonight, do
                the first two and keep this one for when it is.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-adc-homework">
                <title>Homework</title>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-hw-t1">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <term>Move the pot to A3.</term>  Rewire the wiper to the
                            header pin labeled <c>A3</c> and make the program work
                            again.  You will need to find out which port pin
                            <c>A3</c> is, which the Nucleo pinout gives, and then
                            which ADC channel that port pin reaches, which is
                            datasheet Table 12 again.  Do not assume any of the
                            three numbers match.
                            Work each translation out from the pinout and the
                            datasheet, and keep a record of the chain you followed.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Everything that mentions the old pin has to change with
                            it.  Check each line of <c>pa0_adc_init()</c> in turn and
                            ask whether it still names the right thing.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-hw-t2">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <term>Change the resolution.</term>  Set your pot so the
                            DMM reads as close to 1.000 V as you can manage, and
                            record the count the ADC returns.  Then find the <c>RES</c> field in
                            <c>ADC_CFGR1</c> in the reference manual and switch the
                            converter to 10-bit.  Read the field's access rules while
                            you are there: RM0490 §14.12.4 allows software to program
                            <c>ADC_CFGR1</c> only while <c>ADEN</c> is cleared, so
                            <em>where</em> you put this line matters as much as what
                            it says.  Predict the new count before you run it, then
                            check.  Explain the factor you see.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-adc-hw-t3">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <term>Check the arithmetic both ways.</term>  Pick any
                            three knob positions.  For each, measure the wiper with
                            the DMM, record the count your program reports, and
                            compute the voltage from the count.  How closely do the
                            measured and computed voltages agree, and is the
                            difference within the one-LSB rounding you would expect?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-adc-hw-solution" instructor="yes">
                <p>Solution: the A3 chain, and the 10-bit prediction</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><term>[port pin]</term> A3 &#8594; <clr c="orange">PB1</clr> (Nucleo pinout), then <term>[channel]</term> &#8594; <clr c="green">ADC_IN18</clr> (Datasheet Table 12). <em>Not</em> channel 3.</li>
                    <li>Different <em>port</em>, so the port clock changes too.</li>
                    <li>10-bit: the same 1.000 V gives about 1241 / 4 &#8776; <term>310</term> counts.</li>
                </ul>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->IOPENR  |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOBEN;                     // port B, not port A
GPIOB->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE1_Msk;                  // PB1, so MODE1
GPIOB->MODER |= (GPIO_ANALOG << GPIO_MODER_MODE1_Pos);

ADC1->CHSELR  = ADC_CHSELR_CHSEL18;                     // channel 18 -- plain '='
                                                        // so channel 0 is deselected
]]></code></program>
                <note>Three things change, not one &#8212; that is the point of the exercise. Note the plain <c>=</c>: a <c>|=</c> would leave channel 0 selected as well and the ADC would scan a two-channel sequence. Verify <c>ADC_CHSELR_CHSEL18</c> exists in the project's CMSIS header before class &#8212; some STM32 headers stop at CHSEL17.</note>
            </slide>

        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!--  REFERENCE                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <section xml:id="sec-adc-reference">
        <title>Reference: The ADC Hardware</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This section is lookup material for everything we did with the
                ADC.  Nothing here is a new idea; it is the same material arranged
                for the moment you come back needing one fact, with more detail on
                the parts we passed over quickly in class.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-ref-hardware">
            <title>Inside the Converter</title>
            <p>
                A conversion takes a fixed number of ADC clock cycles, and this is
                where they go.
                <xref ref="fig-adc-timing"/> puts the sampling window and the twelve
                comparisons side by side, with the flags the hardware sets at each
                end of them.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-adc-timing">
                <caption>Conversion timing (<url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url>, <em>Analog to digital
                conversion time</em>).  Setting <c>ADSTART</c> starts sampling;
                sampling takes 1.5 ADC clock cycles and the successive
                approximation itself 12.5 (the 1.5 is the reset value of
                <c>SMP1[2:0]</c> in <c>ADC_SMPR</c>, which this chapter leaves alone), so a 12-bit
                conversion is 14 cycles in all, about 1.17 µs at the default 12 MHz ADC clock.  <c>EOC</c> is
                then set and the result is in <c>ADC_DR</c>.  <c>ADSTART</c> stays
                set for the whole conversion and is cleared by hardware at the
                <em>end</em>.  While it reads 1, the converter is busy.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07-ADC/adc-timing.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-ref-impedance">
            <title>Source Resistance and Sampling Time</title>
            <p>
                The ADC does not measure a voltage without drawing anything from it.
                Inside the converter is a small capacitor that must be charged up to
                the input voltage before the successive approximation can begin, and
                it charges <em>through</em> whatever source you have connected,
                through the potentiometer's track, or through a sensor's own output
                resistance.
            </p>
            <p>
                That charging happens inside a fixed window: the sampling time, set
                by <c>SMP1[2:0]</c> in <c>ADC_SMPR</c> and left at its shortest
                setting throughout this chapter.  Note what this does <em>not</em>
                depend on: how often you take a reading.  Sampling once a second
                rather than a thousand times a second gives the capacitor no extra
                time on any individual conversion, because each conversion gets the
                same window.  If the source cannot charge the capacitor within that
                window, every reading comes back low by an amount that does not
                average away.
            </p>
            <p>
                The <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">datasheet</url>'s <em>Maximum ADC RAIN</em> table is the lookup, where
                R<sub>AIN</sub> is the resistance of whatever you have connected to
                the pin: it
                gives the largest source resistance the converter tolerates for a
                given sampling time and resolution.  Read it carefully, since the table is
                indexed at a 35 MHz ADC clock, so a given number of sampling cycles
                buys you nearly three times as long at the 12 MHz clock this course
                runs.  A 10 kΩ potentiometer presents its worst case at mid-travel,
                where the two halves of the track are in parallel: about 2.5 kΩ.
                That is enough to cost a few counts, which is invisible on a knob you
                are turning by hand and very visible on a sensor you are trying to
                trust.  The fix is to lengthen the sampling time, not to sample less
                often.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-adc-ref-registers">
            <title>Register Summary</title>
            <p>
                These are the registers this chapter uses, each with the one field
                of it that we touch.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-adc-registers">
                <title>The ADC registers used in this chapter</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Register</cell><cell>Field</cell><cell>Purpose</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>RCC_APBENR2</c></cell><cell><c>ADCEN</c></cell>
                        <cell>Enable the clock to the ADC</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>GPIOx_MODER</c></cell><cell><c>MODEn</c></cell>
                        <cell>Set the pin to analog mode (<c>11</c>)</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>ADC_CHSELR</c></cell><cell><c>CHSELn</c></cell>
                        <cell>Select channel <m>n</m> for conversion</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>ADC_CR</c></cell><cell><c>ADEN</c></cell>
                        <cell>Enable the converter</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>ADC_CR</c></cell><cell><c>ADSTART</c></cell>
                        <cell>Start a conversion</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>ADC_ISR</c></cell><cell><c>ADRDY</c></cell>
                        <cell>Converter is ready (write 1 to clear)</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>ADC_ISR</c></cell><cell><c>EOC</c></cell>
                        <cell>Conversion complete (cleared by reading <c>ADC_DR</c>, or by writing 1)</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>ADC_DR</c></cell><cell>–</cell>
                        <cell>The 12-bit result, right-aligned in bits 11:0</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><c>ADC_CFGR1</c></cell><cell><c>RES</c></cell>
                        <cell>Resolution: 12, 10, 8, or 6 bits</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
